n investments.^The presence of foreign investors in the debt market can enhance market liquidity, making it easier for issuers to raise capital.^Fluctuations in FPIs can indicate changes in the perceived risk of investing in a country's debt, reflecting factors like political stability, fiscal policy, and global economic conditions."},{"question":"How to interpret the Foreign Portfolio Investments (FPIs) - Debt data?","answer":"A high level of FPIs in Debt often reflects international confidence in a country's economic stability, debt sustainability and the perceived reliability of its debt market.^The levels of FPIs in Debt can reflect the effectiveness of government policies, especially those related to fiscal discipline and economic reform.^Decrease in foreign investment in debt securities could raise concerns about fiscal health.^FPIs in Debt often correlate with other economic indicators, such as GDP growth, inflation rates, and credit ratings, offering a comprehensive view of the economic situation."}],"related":[{"slug":"aggregate-bank-deposits","title":"Aggregate Bank Deposits"},{"slug":"bank-credit-industry","title":"Bank Credit - Industry"},{"slug":"bank-credit-personal-loans","title":"Bank Credit - Personal Loans"},{"slug":"bank-credit-services","title":"Bank Credit - Services"},{"slug":"cash-reserve-ratio-crr","title":"Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)"},{"slug":"credit-deposit-ratio","title":"Credit-Deposit Ratio"},{"slug":"foreign-exchange-reserves","title":"Foreign Exchange Reserves"},{"slug":"marginal-cost-of-funds-based-lending-rates-mclr","title":"Marginal Cost of Funds based Lending Rates (MCLR)"},{"slug":"money-supply-m3","title":"Money Supply (M3)"},{"slug":"nifty-bank-pe-ratio","title":"NIFTY Bank P/E Ratio"},{"slug":"rbi-liquidity-net-laf","title":"RBI Liquidity (Net LAF)"},{"slug":"repo-rate","title":"Repo Rate"},{"slug":"statutory-liquidity-ratio-slr","title":"Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR)"},{"slug":"term-deposit-rate","title":"Term Deposit Rate"},